<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">F. W. de Klerk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–<a href="/years/2021">2021</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Frederik Willem de Klerk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">F. W. de Klerk was the last state president of apartheid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, serving from 1989 to 1994. In February 1990 he unbanned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> and other liberation movements and released <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>, and he negotiated the end of white minority rule. He shared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> with Mandela in 1993 and served as a deputy president in Mandela's government until 1996.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk.jpg" alt="F. W. de Klerk" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carol M. Highsmith · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F.W._de_Klerk_wait_to_speak_in_Philadelphia,_Pennsylvania_LCCN2011634246_(cropped).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
